WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — gates (180s) Narrator George

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.571
First, a computer should not take an irreversible

2
00:00:03.571 --> 00:00:07.109
action until an independent check confirms it is safe.

3
00:00:07.669 --> 00:00:11.105
Second, it should try the cheapest method first and

4
00:00:11.105 --> 00:00:14.542
wake a large AI model only when simpler tools miss.

5
00:00:15.102 --> 00:00:19.939
Third, every decision is written to a receipt with the energy it used,

6
00:00:19.939 --> 00:00:21.942
so anyone can audit it later.

7
00:00:22.502 --> 00:00:30.037
Chatbots can recover from a clumsy sentence by writing another polite paragraph, yet autonomous software tools

8
00:00:30.037 --> 00:00:37.504
cross real-world boundaries that cannot be rolled back once executed. Even cheap per-word AI becomes wasteful

9
00:00:37.504 --> 00:00:45.861
and dangerous when ten unverified guesses corrupt records, trigger expensive rollbacks, and force human cleanup afterward.

10
00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:54.322
Imagine a hospital pharmacy where routine refills follow an exact printed checklist, unusual prescriptions escalate to

11
00:00:54.322 --> 00:01:00.281
a specialist, and a physical barcode lock refuses to open the drawer if dosage or allergy

12
00:01:00.281 --> 00:01:07.245
limits fail. Neither raw speed nor model cleverness ever gets an informal side channel around that gate.

13
00:01:07.805 --> 00:01:15.562
Every irreversible action passes through four strict runtime records in our Rust reference implementation. First comes

14
00:01:15.562 --> 00:01:23.845
an untrusted proposal that cannot authorize itself. Second comes a joint certificate requiring table permission, energy budget

15
00:01:23.845 --> 00:01:31.471
headroom, and optional safety barriers. Third comes commit or typed refusal, and fourth comes a timestamped receipt.

16
00:01:32.031 --> 00:01:39.760
By classic sequential search theory, trying gears in decreasing order of pass probability divided by energy cost

17
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:48.386
minimizes expected spend across every workload. Measuring total energy to correct completion penalizes every failed retry and

18
00:01:48.386 --> 00:01:56.597
refusal, showing clearly why spending picojoules on early deterministic checks saves whole joules of wasted model runs.

19
00:01:57.157 --> 00:02:04.333
This three-dimensional hardware cutaway shows eight parallel safety gates checking rule tables, energy budgets, and

20
00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:11.946
safety barriers together at twenty-five megahertz on reconfigurable silicon, while an onboard circuit-board current sensor

21
00:02:11.946 --> 00:02:19.309
measures real module supply power across interleaved start-and-stop runs and matches software decisions step for step.

22
00:02:19.869 --> 00:02:27.344
Across two hundred nine thousand two hundred sixty-one fixed-point control checks, the discrete gate recorded zero

23
00:02:27.344 --> 00:02:35.801
unsafe approvals and only seventy-nine cautious refusals, while blocking unsafe external tool calls before execution. Interleaved

24
00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:44.390
hardware board-sensor measurements recorded eleven thousand four hundred twenty-eight picojoules per gate cycle across four passes.

25
00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:52.932
Every energy figure states plainly how it was obtained and what remains unmeasured. Our eleven-thousand-picojoule hardware

26
00:02:52.932 --> 00:03:00.064
reading spans the whole vision module supply rather than an isolated core rail, a tiny ninety-eight-picojoule

27
00:03:00.064 --> 00:03:08.504
seed test stayed inconclusive near sensor resolution, and software operation counts stay clearly labeled as analytical estimates.
